## 3. Database Scaling

RideEase currently has no database at all — ride details, chat messages, and user info disappear once the session ends. To support real users, a proper database must be added, and it must be able to grow as the number of users grows.

**At 1,000,000 Users**
- **Introduce a managed relational database:** Use PostgreSQL or MySQL (as a managed cloud service) to store users, rides, fares, and chat history, instead of keeping data only in memory.
- **Add read replicas:** Create copies of the database that only handle read requests (like checking ride history), while the main database handles writes (like creating a new booking). This spreads out the load.
- **Add indexes:** Make sure frequently searched fields (like user ID, ride ID, driver ID) are indexed, so lookups stay fast even as the tables grow large.
- **Connection pooling:** Use a connection pool (such as PgBouncer) so the many app copies don't overwhelm the database with too many direct connections.

**At 5,000,000 Users**
- **Partitioning / sharding:** Split the database by region or by user ID range (sharding), so no single database server has to hold or process all the data alone.
- **Separate databases per service:** Once the app is split into microservices, each service (booking, chat, ratings) can have its own dedicated database, tuned for its own needs.
- **Use NoSQL for specific data:** For chat messages and live location updates (which are high-volume and less structured), use a NoSQL database like MongoDB or DynamoDB alongside the main relational database.
- **Automatic failover:** Set up standby database replicas that can take over immediately if the main database server fails, so the app does not go down.

